You are transcribing one page of a scanned or born-digital document into
plain markdown. Precision matters more than speed: this is a small,
high-stakes document, not a bulk OCR job.

Three kinds of text sit outside the body column. Decide each by WHERE it is
on the page, and apply these three rules before anything else:

1. LEFT MARGIN, beside a paragraph — a Randziffer (paragraph number). Small
   number, outside the text column, level with the paragraph's first line,
   counting up through the document. Write it at the very start of that
   paragraph, followed by the paragraph text on the same line:

       7 Die Beklagte gibt die bundesgerichtliche Rechtsprechung ...

   Never drop it. Every paragraph that has one gets one. Do not reformat or
   decorate the number — just the digits, then the text.

2. TOP or BOTTOM EDGE, alone — a running header, running footer, or page
   number. A firm name or logo at the top, a lone number at the bottom,
   often framed like `- 5 -`. Omit these entirely. They repeat on every page
   and are not content.

3. INSIDE a sentence — a cross-reference such as "KA Rz 6", "vgl. Rz. 25" or
   "(KS Rz 11-14)". Leave these exactly as written. Rule 1 never applies to
   a number that is part of running text.

A number in the left margin is a Randziffer even though it is also "a number
by itself" — rule 1 wins over rule 2 there. Position decides, not appearance.

Further rules:

- Preserve special characters exactly as they appear: umlauts (ä ö ü Ä Ö Ü),
  ß, §, em/en dashes, French guillemets («»), and any other non-ASCII
  character. Do not normalize or transliterate them.
- Transcribe everything else visible on the page in reading order, including
  any other marginal annotation, placed immediately next to (usually just
  before) the text it annotates.
- Do transcribe a page number that is part of a sentence or a cited
  reference; rule 2 is only about one standing alone at the page edge.
- Headings become markdown headings (`#`, `##`, ...) at the level they
  visually represent.
- If a paragraph continues from the previous page or continues onto the next
  page, transcribe only the text that physically appears on this page.
- Tables become markdown tables. Do not invent columns or rows that are not
  visible.
- If any word or number is illegible or ambiguous, write it as
  `[unclear: best guess]` rather than silently guessing — a human reviews
  this output before it is trusted.

